WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.819
WHAT is COREPER?

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:07.609
You may not have heard of Coreper, but it
is an important body at the heart of EU policy-making.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:12.520
Coreper stands for the Committee of the Permanent
Representatives of the 28 governments that

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.970
make up the European Union.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.509
It prepares the work that allows Europe's
ministers and leaders to take important policy

00:00:19.509 --> 00:00:23.800
decisions that impact on the lives of Europe's
500 million citizens.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.380
WHO works in COREPER?

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.140
Coreper is made up of the permanent representatives
- ambassadors - and their deputies from the

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.230
member states. They help ensure that national
concerns are taken into account in European

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.290
policy-making.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.660
Formally they meet in Brussels once a week,
but in reality the representatives talk to

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.660
each other every day.
Their sessions are held in two formats - Coreper

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:52.660
2, composed of the permanent representatives,
and Coreper 1, made up of their deputies.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.470
Together, these two formats cover all the
different EU policy areas.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:57.960
WHAT do they do exactly?

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.780
Their job is to prepare the meetings of the
Council of the European Union.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.510
The Council is the body where ministers discuss,
debate and decide EU policy on topics ranging

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:13.440
from fisheries to safety legislation, from
the banking union to trade.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.060
Decision-making is left to ministers, but
Coreper plays an important role in preparing

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.360
the choices for them.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.659
Through its work, Coreper identifies areas
of agreement and disagreement between different

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:27.390
national positions.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:32.280
It tries to find ways for differences to be
resolved, and clarifies the choices for ministers

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.890
ahead of their meetings.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.320
Coreper is also tasked with implementing ministers'
and leaders' instructions.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.530
HOW does it all work then?

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.950
Work often begins weeks or months before the
Council meeting.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:50.970
Discussion is based on the preliminary efforts
of more than 150 working groups and committees.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.450
Throughout its work, Coreper members are in
constant contact with their national governments

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.909
in their capitals.

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:02.740
Quite often, all this careful preparation
leads to an outcome that is generally agreed.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.610
In these cases, discussion agreed in Coreper
can become a so-called 'A' item on the Council

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:09.800
agenda.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.420
That means it is passed to ministers for decision
without further debate, unless a country specifically

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.450
requests one.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:23.000
Sometimes Coreper cannot reach agreement because
more technical work is required. In this

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.340
case, it can send the issue back to the experts
in the working parties.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.790
For many issues however, reaching an agreement
requires ministers to make choices and give

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.400
political input.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.290
These issues are sent by Coreper as so-called
'B' items on the Council agenda, for discussion

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.640
at ministerial level.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.569
So that, in a nutshell, is Coreper. For more
information, take a look at the Council's

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:48.129
website.

